+ {"status": "in_progress", "id": "AUG-131", "title": "Namespace teardown fails when a concurrent indexer recreates the directory (ENOTEMPTY)", "detail": {"fingerprint": "2e16d51daaf57b84f05184cb842dc66f2053d8b607c7f006f132d5774c947a4a"}, "priority": "P1", "source": null, "reasoning": "Found 2026-11-03 while landing AUG-125..AUG-130: two consecutive guarded commits failed the suite's leak audit. tests/test_auger.py::test_no_test_namespace_survives_the_suite reported auger-pytest-4bcbdb98-5c32565373a2 (then auger-pytest-ff50909c-504ca4427450) left in BOTH the registry and on disk. First failure came from DELETE /api/namespaces/<ns> returning 500 'Failed to remove namespace directory: ENOTEMPTY'. Re-deleting 41 leftover test namespaces removed them and 23 reappeared within seconds, each holding an empty .embeddings/ directory and zero files, so a DuckBrain background writer recreates a deleted namespace. Measured registry: 310 rows, 25 auger-pytest-* debris, 285 real namespaces (no real data affected). A controlled probe of 6 create/write/delete cycles returned 200 and removed the tree 6/6, so this is a race window, not a deterministic break: the deletion is only unsafe while an index write is in flight. Fix shipped in conftest.teardown_namespace: a settled DELETE now gets a bounded removal retry budget (REMOVAL_BACKOFFS, sum ~1.9s) that re-issues the DELETE when the tree comes back, instead of reporting the race as a leak on the first attempt. Proved hermetically in tests/test_teardown_resilience.py (4 tests: recreated twice is still removed; always-recreated is reported and bounded; a refused DELETE still keeps directory+row per AUG-117; a real namespace name is refused), now named in tests/gate.sh's pytest arm. DuckBrain-side root cause (indexer must cancel or await pending writes on delete) is filed against DuckBrain.", "review_notes": null, "ts": null, "acceptance_criteria": "PASS: the hermetic teardown tests run in tests/gate.sh's arm and fail if the retry budget is removed (mutation: delete REMOVAL_BACKOFFS handling, expect red). PASS: a live suite run whose namespace directory is recreated after DELETE still ends with the leak audit green, and a tree that never disappears is still reported as residue rather than silently passed. PASS: a refused DELETE (409/429/5xx) still keeps BOTH the registry row and the directory (AUG-117 unchanged), proved by the fourth test. PASS: the retry is bounded — worst case added wall time per namespace is under 2s, asserted by the attempt-count test.", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-03T20:47:35.882981+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-11-07T01:38:13.131765+00:00", "foreman_note": "DRIVER MEASURED 2026-11-06 at b93630b: the shipped fix is already on main and load-bearing — tests/conftest.py REMOVAL_BACKOFFS=(0.0,0.1,0.25,0.5,1.0) (sum ~1.9s, <2s AC), re-issues DELETE when the tree reappears; tests/test_teardown_resilience.py 4 tests pass in 2.21s ('....'), and the file IS named in tests/gate.sh's pytest arm (line 178). Not closed: no commit-bound GitReins verdict exists (verdict debt = AUG-154), so no evidence-run-id — none fabricated.\n\nRE-MEASURED 2026-11-06 during SG10 closeout: the bounded retry (REMOVAL_BACKOFFS sum ~1.9s) is shorter than a DuckBrain native-sync push window. Merged-main gate run 1 at c6ed304 was rc 1 with 3 failed / 12 errors / 647 passed in 1899.50s, EVERY one of them a teardown DELETE 409 'Push in flight ... native sync lock held by pid 1970051', which left 14 auger-pytest-ae536f42-* namespaces behind (registry rows AND directories, the correct refused-DELETE behaviour) and failed test_no_test_namespace_survives_the_suite. Run 2 on the SAME tree (lock holder gone, 14 namespaces reclaimed via auger.delete_namespace after proving /tmp/auger-pytest-substrate.lock free) was rc 0, 650 passed / 2 skipped in 929.31s. So a long suite's verdict is currently load/lock-clock dependent; the retry budget is the residual this row owns. Logs: ~/.hermes/goals/sg10-gate-main.log (r